      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the nature of the roots and how they contribute to the polynomial. The given roots are \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\). For a polynomial with rational coefficients, if a number \\(a + \\sqrt{b}\\) is a root, then its conjugate \\(a - \\sqrt{b}\\) must also be a root. \n\nThus, for each given root \\(k + \\sqrt{k+1}\\), the conjugate \\(k - \\sqrt{k+1}\\) must also be a root. \n\nThe roots are of the form \\(k + \\sqrt{k+1}\\) for \\(k = 1, 2, \\dots, 1000\\). Therefore, for each \\(k\\), the polynomial must have both \\(k + \\sqrt{k+1}\\) and \\(k - \\sqrt{k+1}\\) as roots. \n\nThis means that the polynomial must have at least \\(1000\\) roots of the form \\(k + \\sqrt{k+1}\\) and at least another \\(1000\\) roots of the form \\(k - \\sqrt{k+1}\\), since each \\(k + \\sqrt{k+1}\\) has a corresponding \\(k - \\sqrt{k+1}\\).\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is \\(1000 + 1000 = 2000\\).\n\nThe reasoning is that we need at least one root for each of the \\(1000\\) double roots, so the polynomial must have up to \\(2 \\times 1000 = 2000\\) roots in total, which is the smallest possible degree.\n\nLet's verify this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\n# The function to find the smallest possible degree of the polynomial\ndef smallest_possible_degree():\n    # Number of given roots\n    num_roots = 1000\n    # Each root k + sqrt(k+1) and its conjugate k - sqrt(k+1) contributes to the polynomial\n    degree = 2 * num_roots\n    return degree\n\n# Calculate the smallest possible degree\nsmallest_degree = smallest_possible_degree()\nprint(smallest_degree)\n```\n```output\n2000\n```\nThe smallest possible degree of a nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients that has all of the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).",
