      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the smallest possible degree of a polynomial with rational coefficients that has all of the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we must consider the properties of polynomials with rational coefficients. Specifically, if a polynomial with rational coefficients has an irrational root, then its conjugate must also be a root of the polynomial.\n\nThe given roots are \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\). For each of these roots, the conjugate \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2-\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3-\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\) must also be a root of the polynomial.\n\nSince there are 1000 such pairs of conjugate roots (from \\(1+\\sqrt{2}\\) to \\(1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\)), the polynomial must have at least \\(2 \\times 1000 = 2000\\) roots.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).",
